From e5e841ef95c8d5c9bdf3ce9ef94285d87b07f746 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <sacredtrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 23:09:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] JSC: WebKit's Ruby generators as Python, so the 68k build
 needs no Ruby

offlineasm (the front end and the M68K back end), the bytecode generator
and GeneratePreferences run as Python 3 ports, standard library only:
Source/JavaScriptCore/offlineasm-py, Source/JavaScriptCore/generator-py and
Source/WTF/Scripts/GeneratePreferences.py. BytecodeList.rb is read as it is
by a reader for the small part of Ruby it uses; the preferences template has
a Python form (.pyerb) and the YAML file is read by a subset reader.

On AmigaOS CMake runs them with python3 and does not look for Ruby. Air's
opcode generator is skipped while B3 is off. The 68k build always uses the
M68K back end (the JSC_M68K_LLINT option is gone; the C-loop back end has no
port and the build stops if another back end is asked for). A step that
still asks for Ruby (WebCore's generators, not ported yet) runs no-ruby.py,
which names the script and fails.

The outputs match the Ruby generators' byte for byte on the 6 October M68K
build's inputs (LLIntAssembly.h, LLIntDesiredOffsets.h,
LLIntDesiredSettings.h, InitBytecodes.asm, Bytecodes.h, BytecodeStructs.h,
BytecodeIndices.h, BytecodeDumperGenerated.cpp, JSCWebPreferenceOptions.h),
apart from the hash of the generator's own files in the LLInt headers.
